This is Frank Defratis for Holotalk.

17
00:02:03,240 --> 00:02:05,640
Have a great week.

18
00:02:05,640 --> 00:02:13,880
Before I begin the actual presentation segment of this recording, I would like to take a moment to outline the segments that we'll be following.

19
00:02:13,880 --> 00:02:26,040
First, I would like to give a brief view of holography from a historical perspective, including how holography moved from the large government-funded research facilities into the home hobbyist environment.

20
00:02:26,040 --> 00:02:32,120
Moving on from there, I would like to cover the main components of the practice of making holograms.

21
00:02:32,120 --> 00:02:41,480
In the third segment, I will give a breakdown of the main types and styles of holograms currently available and their main similarities and differences.

22
00:02:41,480 --> 00:02:47,080
In the final segment, we'll cover where holography is today and where it will be tomorrow.

23
00:02:47,080 --> 00:03:00,360
So let us now move into our first segment, holography from a historical perspective.

24
00:03:00,360 --> 00:03:12,120
Despite the widely held belief that holograms are a new technological innovation, the history of holography actually began in 1948, many years before the invention of the laser.

25
00:03:12,120 --> 00:03:15,720
It was at the Imperial College of London that a researcher named Dr.

26
00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:20,200
Dennis Gabor conceived of and produced the first hologram.

27
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:31,320
The academic paper which he published entitled Image Formation by Reconstructed Wavefronts went on to earn him the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1971.

28
00:03:31,320 --> 00:03:31,800
Dr.

29
00:03:31,800 --> 00:03:37,160
Gabor used a carefully filtered sodium vapor lamp to make his early holograms.

30
00:03:37,160 --> 00:03:42,040
This limited the depth of the holograms to only the thickness of a postage stamp.

31
00:03:42,040 --> 00:03:44,760
But it worked, and the rest is history.

32
00:03:46,320 --> 00:03:57,360
The actual intent of his research was to store an image which was illuminated with very short waves such as X-rays, and then play the image back using very long waves.

33
00:03:57,360 --> 00:04:08,160
In essence, this would allow for great magnifications and improvement on the scanning electron microscope, which already could show tremendous detail of whatever was being imaged.

34
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:16,480
It wasn't until the invention of the laser twelve years later that holography began to explore the beginnings of its potential.

35
00:04:16,480 --> 00:04:24,480
Today, even 1960 seems like ages ago, but it was the year of the invention of a device that would create a revolution.

36
00:04:24,480 --> 00:04:27,280
Of course, I am referring to the laser.

37
00:04:27,280 --> 00:04:34,320
The word laser is actually an acronym for the term's light amplification by stimulated emission of radiation.

38
00:04:34,320 --> 00:04:39,200
And at the time of its invention, there really wasn't a use for it.

39
00:04:39,200 --> 00:04:47,440
That was a very interesting time in our history, for here we are, as a race, creating a tool before anyone had a use for it.

40
00:04:47,440 --> 00:04:52,160
It was actually the first time in human history that this had been so.

41
00:04:52,160 --> 00:04:57,600
In fact, the laser was termed a solution in search of a problem.

42
00:04:57,600 --> 00:05:02,960
But there were researchers hard at work with the theories of Dennis Gabor at the time.

43
00:05:02,960 --> 00:05:11,760
Yuri Denishuk of the former Soviet Union, and Emmett Leith and Jure Supetniks at the University of Michigan in the United States.

44
00:05:11,760 --> 00:05:26,800
These researchers took Gabor's theories, applied them using the special properties of light that only a laser can produce, and created the first true three-dimensional images we now call holograms.

45
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:33,640
The scientific journals were absolutely filled with articles for years after the initial first holograms were created.

46
00:05:34,120 --> 00:05:39,000
It was actually seen as one of the most exciting inventions of this century.

47
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:53,800
Not only articles and academic papers, but on the legal front, literally hundreds of patents were issued as research laboratories worked not only round the clock, but straight through the calendar year to come up with uses for this new technology.

48
00:05:53,800 --> 00:06:04,920
As the 60s began to come to a close, corporate interest began to wane, especially at the larger facilities that were receiving much of the defense spending on research and development.

49
00:06:04,920 --> 00:06:09,880
After nearly a decade, practical uses appeared to be on the way out.

50
00:06:09,880 --> 00:06:15,720
Fortunately, most of the excitement, at least for the everyday person, was just beginning.

51
00:06:15,720 --> 00:06:22,680
The mysteries that were surrounding the field like a curtain began to fade away, exposing the field to millions.

52
00:06:22,680 --> 00:06:31,720
One of the greatest revelations to come out of this transition was the fact that holography was not necessarily out of the reach of the amateur or hobbyist.

53
00:06:31,720 --> 00:06:40,840
Most felt that the field was too technical and expensive, primarily because of the fact that it was only being practiced in larger research laboratories.

54
00:06:40,840 --> 00:06:43,640
Both of these assumptions were incorrect.

55
00:06:43,640 --> 00:06:52,840
As far as too technical is concerned, most photographers do not understand the optical mathematics that go along with just the simplest camera.

56
00:06:52,840 --> 00:06:57,560
There's no reason to expect amateur holographers to do so either.

57
00:06:57,560 --> 00:07:17,040
Basic theory is really all that's needed here, and as far as the cost of equipment is concerned, if one has a basic understanding of the general theories behind the field, a person can actually make a home holography laboratory or studio, if you will, out of materials readily available from most home centers.

58
00:07:14,840 --> 00:07:19,280
And that is exactly what happened.

59
00:07:19,520 --> 00:07:25,040
Some of these homemade studios actually rivaled the larger laboratories in quality.

60
00:07:25,040 --> 00:07:27,280
A few actually surpassed it.

61
00:07:27,280 --> 00:07:34,960
And keep in mind the savings of thousands of dollars off what the larger laboratories were paying for equipment.

62
00:07:34,960 --> 00:07:37,760
There's no doubt that it was a revolution.

63
00:07:37,760 --> 00:07:41,920
It created quite a bit of noise in the professional circles as well.

64
00:07:41,920 --> 00:07:57,520
I mean, tell me, how do you explain the person on the other side of town doing holograms in their basement that are actually comparable or even sometimes better in quality than the holograms you've been making on a system that just cost your company thousands of dollars.

65
00:07:57,520 --> 00:08:02,640
It was happening all over, and it is still happening today.

66
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:16,160
There are three major areas of any holographic setup, whether it's a professional studio laboratory at a research facility or a home hobbyist setup.

67
00:08:16,160 --> 00:08:28,960
There is the actual laser, the holography table, which contains the lenses and mirrors used to make the hologram, and the darkroom where the development of the finished holographic plate takes place.

68
00:08:28,960 --> 00:08:33,920
The laser is a light-producing device that projects a beam of light.

69
00:08:33,920 --> 00:08:38,240
The light that is produced by a laser has very special properties.

70
00:08:38,240 --> 00:08:42,240
It is these special properties that allow us to make a hologram.

71
00:08:42,240 --> 00:08:47,920
As of this date today, the laser is the only device that can produce this light.

72
00:08:47,920 --> 00:08:54,400
Lasers run in size from the size of a football field or larger to the size of a grain of salt.

73
00:08:54,400 --> 00:08:59,520
The most common laser used in the field of holography is the helium neon laser.

74
00:08:59,720 --> 00:09:02,120
Other lasers are used as well.

75
00:09:02,120 --> 00:09:10,280
Argon lasers, helium cadmium lasers, as well as pulsed Ruby lasers are also used by some hologers.

76
00:09:10,280 --> 00:09:25,000
The diode laser, which is a tiny laser found in many devices such as CD players, are starting to make an appearance in a few laboratories for producing holograms, but as of this date are still in the research stage.

77
00:09:30,680 --> 00:09:37,400
Most recording medium, such as holographic plates, are produced in large quantities for this laser.

78
00:09:37,400 --> 00:09:48,200
It also has the lowest cost of all the lasers, and it has the longest life with nearly a 20,000-hour maintenance-free lifetime on the actual tube.

79
00:10:09,080 --> 00:10:15,000
This electrical current causes the gases in the tube to glow brightly.

80
00:10:30,320 --> 00:10:36,080
End of the tube, a mirror which is not quite 100% reflective is placed.

81
00:10:36,080 --> 00:10:45,200
Because this mirror does not reflect 100% of the light striking it back through the tube, a small percentage of the light passes through it.

82
00:10:45,200 --> 00:10:49,920
This is the end of the tube that the actual laser beam exits the laser.

83
00:10:49,920 --> 00:10:56,800
Because of the shape of the mirrors, they line up the light into a straight line, which is perceived as a beam.

84
00:10:56,800 --> 00:11:01,280
The actual physical construction of a helium neon laser is quite simple.

85
00:11:01,280 --> 00:11:08,080
However, it is critical that the components are at specific distances from each other, especially the mirrors.

86
00:11:08,080 --> 00:11:13,760
They are placed at an exact multiple of the specific wave front the laser is producing.

87
00:11:13,760 --> 00:11:19,200
Each color of the rainbow, from red through violet, has a specific wavelength.

88
00:11:19,360 --> 00:11:24,320
It is the difference in the measurement of the wavelength that gives us the different colors.

89
00:11:24,320 --> 00:11:31,600
The helium neon laser produces a red beam, so it is said that it operates at the red end of the spectrum.

90
00:11:31,600 --> 00:11:36,960
The actual wavelength measurement is stated in the values which are called nanometers.

91
00:11:36,960 --> 00:11:49,920
The exact measurement of the wavelength that a helium neon laser produces is 632.8 nanometers, or rounded off, 633 nanometers.

92
00:11:49,920 --> 00:11:56,560
There are lasers which produce several distinct wavelengths, and lasers which produce only one at a time.

93
00:11:56,560 --> 00:12:06,080
A helium neon laser can also be made to produce colors in both the yellow and green parts of the spectrum, but it can only produce one color at a time.

94
00:12:06,080 --> 00:12:11,120
This trait of producing only one color at a time is called monochromatic.

95
00:12:11,120 --> 00:12:19,840
Common household light bulbs produce nearly all the colors of the spectrum and are therefore the furthest thing from monochromatic that you can get.

96
00:12:19,840 --> 00:12:25,520
Having a monochromatic light source is an important part of producing a hologram.

97
00:12:25,520 --> 00:12:30,840
The other special property of a laser is that the light it produces is termed coherent.

98
00:12:29,920 --> 00:12:34,600
This is the trait that really started the ball rolling in holography.

99
00:12:34,840 --> 00:12:40,040
A coherent light source has all of the waves that it produces in line with one another.

100
00:12:40,040 --> 00:12:42,520
I like to call it a wave train.

101
00:12:42,520 --> 00:12:46,600
Each wave of light is perfectly in step with one another.

102
00:12:46,600 --> 00:12:52,360
For comparison, an ordinary household light bulb is an incoherent source of light.

103
00:12:52,360 --> 00:12:58,280
All of the waves that it produces leave the light bulb in a random, haphazard fashion.

104
00:12:58,280 --> 00:13:11,400
A good example that I like to use to help people visualize this information is I ask them to picture themselves in a helicopter, stationary over the top of a crowd of people at an outdoor concert.

105
00:13:11,400 --> 00:13:21,720
Looking down, you see thousands of people, each dressed in different colors and haphazardly standing around in no specific, well-defined pattern.

106
00:13:21,720 --> 00:13:25,960
This would be the light that a household light bulb would produce.

107
00:13:25,960 --> 00:13:33,080
Now I ask them to take their helicopter and hover over the parade, marching down the main street of town.

108
00:13:33,080 --> 00:13:40,280
The band is moving in unison with one another, marching forward, all dressed in red uniforms.

109
00:13:40,280 --> 00:13:49,960
This is the light of a laser, one color or monochromatic and coherent, all waves moving along, one right behind the other.

110
00:13:49,960 --> 00:13:56,120
I hope this helps you get the picture in your mind of the difference between ordinary light and laser light.

111
00:13:56,120 --> 00:14:04,040
And yes, you can land your helicopter and go have an ice cream.

112
00:14:12,680 --> 00:14:28,400
There can be no vibrations to the system whatsoever when making a hologram, so special tables must either be constructed or purchased, which absorb all vibrations before they reach the top of the table where the hologram is being made.

113
00:14:28,400 --> 00:14:33,360
The industry term for such a table is a vibration isolation system.

114
00:14:33,360 --> 00:14:40,320
The usual sizes for a table of this type is either 4x4 feet or 4x8 feet.

115
00:14:40,320 --> 00:14:51,120
These tables can be purchased from scientific supply companies, or they can be built using commonly found materials from local home centers or building supply companies.

116
00:14:51,120 --> 00:15:10,720
Most purchase systems are composed of three major components a honeycomb tabletop, which has a core of honeycomb material and a stainless steel flat top, cylinder legs, which floats the massive tabletop on a cushion of air, and an air compressor, which supplies the legs with the air for flotation.

117
00:15:10,720 --> 00:15:15,600
For high-budget government research, these tables are the best that money can buy.

118
00:15:15,600 --> 00:15:23,200
But we have to ask ourselves, what did the researchers use before there was holography in order to invent it?

119
00:15:23,200 --> 00:15:26,560
Well, the answer is they constructed their own.

120
00:15:26,560 --> 00:15:30,400
Most amateur and hobbyist holograph their own tables.

121
00:15:30,400 --> 00:15:38,400
The materials are easy to locate, and the cost is very inexpensive compared to a purchased system from a scientific supplier.

122
00:15:38,400 --> 00:15:47,560
Although the actual construction of a home table system is easy, it's beyond the scope of this tape to go into the details.

123
00:15:47,840 --> 00:15:53,720
Well, now you have a laser and a vibration isolation table and you're ready to make holograms.

124
00:15:53,880 --> 00:15:56,760
You plug in your laser and you're ready to go.

125
00:15:56,760 --> 00:16:03,080
You'll still need a few lenses and mirrors, and of course, holographic plates to actually put the images on.

126
00:16:03,080 --> 00:16:09,160
There are many different types of holograms you can make, and I would like to cover a few of the styles here with you.

127
00:16:09,160 --> 00:16:15,720
But before I do, I would like to give my choices for these instructional books on making holograms.

128
00:16:15,720 --> 00:16:27,720
First, the book which I consider the definitive amateur's guide to making holograms is a manual titled The Holography Handbook, published by Ross Books in Berkeley, California.

129
00:16:27,720 --> 00:16:35,400
The second, for those of you further into the field, is practical holography, published by Prentice Hall.

130
00:16:35,400 --> 00:16:47,400
If you are planning on doing your own holograms, I want you to be successful, and you cannot go wrong with either of these books for their practical advice and time-tested techniques.

131
00:16:47,400 --> 00:16:56,600
As I mentioned before, there are many types of holograms out there, and I would like to take a few moments to describe what it is you are looking at when you see one.

132
00:16:59,880 --> 00:17:04,120
Laser transmission holograms are the granddaddies of all holograms.

133
00:17:04,120 --> 00:17:08,600
They require a laser to make one and also a laser to view one.

134
00:17:08,600 --> 00:17:14,600
They are rarely seen outside the laboratory environment, however, and this is very unfortunate.

135
00:17:14,600 --> 00:17:22,360
If you ever want to get an idea of exactly what holography is capable of doing, make or view a laser transmission hologram.

136
00:17:22,360 --> 00:17:24,360
It will astound you.

137
00:17:24,360 --> 00:17:28,840
A second type of hologram is a white light transmission hologram.

138
00:17:28,840 --> 00:17:38,120
These holograms require a laser to make them, but they are viewable in ordinary white light situations, such as the sun or a light bulb.

139
00:17:38,120 --> 00:17:44,520
You may recognize them by the way they seem to change colors and appear to give a rainbow effect when viewing them.

140
00:17:44,720 --> 00:17:48,560
Actually, another term for them is rainbow holograms.

141
00:17:48,560 --> 00:18:00,480
But the laser transmission hologram and the white light transmission hologram are viewable with the source of light passing through the hologram from behind it and then traveling to the viewer's eye.

142
00:18:00,480 --> 00:18:07,520
Most of the holograms that are found on consumer products are white light transmission holograms.

143
00:18:07,520 --> 00:18:20,080
However, they have a silver backing, which acts like a mirror, so that when you view them, the light source does not have to travel through the hologram, but can come from the same side as the viewer.

144
00:18:20,080 --> 00:18:28,560
The silver backing reflects this light back through the hologram, as if the light were coming from behind and traveling through.

145
00:18:28,560 --> 00:18:40,160
A very simple, ingenious device that fools the hologram into thinking that the light is coming from behind, when actually it is coming from the same side as the viewer.

146
00:18:40,160 --> 00:18:43,280
This leads us to a reflection hologram.

147
00:18:43,280 --> 00:18:50,720
Reflection hologram replays itself with the light coming from the same side as the viewer, hence the term reflection.

148
00:18:50,720 --> 00:18:54,400
It reflects the light back to the eyes of the viewer.

149
00:18:54,400 --> 00:19:06,960
However, unlike the silver backed white light transmission holograms, with a reflection hologram, the actual hologram reflects the light back to the viewer, not a silver backing.

150
00:19:06,960 --> 00:19:11,280
Reflection holograms are easy and fun to make for the home hobbyist.

151
00:19:11,280 --> 00:19:18,560
However, they require a bit more stability in your system than the transmission type of hologram.

152
00:19:18,560 --> 00:19:24,160
Another type of hologram that seems to be making a comeback is the multiplex hologram.

153
00:19:24,160 --> 00:19:31,000
Also known as a stereogram, these holograms are actually pieces of film which are contained in the cylinder.

154
00:19:29,600 --> 00:19:35,400
The cylinder rotates and the holographic image appears to move.

155
00:19:35,400 --> 00:19:47,080
This movement is made possible by the fact that the original image was first recorded onto standard motion picture film and then transferred in a special way onto the holographic film.

156
00:19:47,080 --> 00:19:58,360
I like to call them pseudo-holograms because of the fact that they were first shot onto a traditional media such as movie film and then transferred and made into a hologram.

157
00:19:58,360 --> 00:20:05,480
However, the effect is always entertaining, and some very interesting images have been created in this fashion.

158
00:20:05,480 --> 00:20:20,120
Some simple types of multiplex holograms can be made at home by the hobbyist, but the commercial quality multiplex requires quite a bit of equipment and a fairly high degree of skill and knowledge to pull it off.

159
00:20:25,480 --> 00:20:33,880
Automobile manufacturers are using holography to test their engines with a technique that is called holographic interferometry.

160
00:20:33,880 --> 00:20:43,000
Turbine engines, medical implants, and numerous consumer products are tested this way to see how each reacts under stress situations.

161
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:52,040
Commercial airline pilots are using holography to view their instrumentation by projecting it out into space in front of the aircraft.

162
00:20:52,040 --> 00:20:58,520
This way they do not have to take their eyes off the sky to see the status of their most important instruments.

163
00:20:58,520 --> 00:21:06,600
Automobile manufacturers will be including this device, known as a heads-up display, in cars in the near future.

164
00:21:06,600 --> 00:21:10,360
Some cars have it as an option right now.

165
00:21:10,360 --> 00:21:14,840
Can we expect to see our friends and loved ones projected into our homes in the future?

166
00:21:15,280 --> 00:21:27,040
The possibility is certainly there, as what is termed real-time holography is being worked on at such facilities as the Media Laboratory at Massachusetts Institute of Technology.

167
00:21:27,040 --> 00:21:36,880
This will allow holograms to be transmitted in real time without any time delay from when the hologram is made to when it is viewed.

168
00:21:36,880 --> 00:21:43,760
One of the biggest explosions holography has made to date has been in the graphic arts industry.

169
00:21:43,760 --> 00:21:55,280
Holograms are beginning to appear on a regular basis on magazine covers, advertisements, on consumer packaging, and even on articles of clothing.

170
00:21:55,280 --> 00:22:05,200
It is a well-documented fact that including a hologram on a product or including a hologram in an advertisement will increase the consumer's response.

171
00:22:05,200 --> 00:22:11,680
The major drawback to most users of holography or potential users is the cost factor.

172
00:22:11,680 --> 00:22:18,560
Setup charges for creating a hologram to be used in this fashion is currently cost prohibitive for most.

173
00:22:18,560 --> 00:22:30,080
However, recent advances in creating holographic masters, which are used to reproduce the millions of holographic copies, might be moving this cost into an acceptable range.

174
00:22:30,080 --> 00:22:37,600
These types of holograms can actually be produced using a desktop computer and a specially fitted laser printer.

175
00:22:37,600 --> 00:22:51,360
At this point in the game, several producers are experimenting with this method, but the majority of consumer-related graphic arts holograms are still being produced in the traditional but costly manner.

176
00:22:51,360 --> 00:22:58,880
Because of the difficulty reproducing holograms, many companies have found them invaluable as a deterrent to counterfeiters.

177
00:22:58,880 --> 00:23:04,520
Most major credit cards have holograms on them, and the possibility of including them on U.S.

178
00:22:59,920 --> 00:23:07,240
currency is currently being reviewed.

179
00:23:07,480 --> 00:23:15,640
Out of all the currency in the world, United States paper is currently near the top of the list when it comes to ease of reproduction.

180
00:23:15,640 --> 00:23:21,720
Recently, a major countyfeeding operation was closed, which was producing counterfeit holograms.

181
00:23:21,720 --> 00:23:25,640
But as far as we know, this was an isolated incident.

182
00:23:25,640 --> 00:23:32,920
Holograms are still by far the best insurance against counterfeiters known today.

183
00:23:33,880 --> 00:23:53,960
As the world continues to discover the magic of holography, and as more and more applications which seem like science fiction only a few years ago become reality, we will continue little by little to view the standards of imaging which we feel is so high-tech and advanced with less and less excitement.

184
00:23:53,960 --> 00:24:02,120
The day will come when it will seem almost primitive to see an image which does not appear three-dimensionally in front of us.

185
00:24:02,120 --> 00:24:13,720
The wide-screen, high-definition digital TVs, which give us such an astounding picture of the world around us, will suddenly seem flat and missing something.

186
00:24:13,720 --> 00:24:36,640
They will be sold or taken up to the resting place in the attic while we actively enjoy beautiful, full color, three-dimensional concerts, sporting events, and yes, even romantic rendezvous, which will be so lifelike that not only will it seem like we're there, we actually will be.

187
00:24:36,360 --> 00:24:42,400
I am happy for your forward and future mindedness with your interest in the field of holography.

188
00:24:42,640 --> 00:24:47,840
For me, it has been literally a lifetime of adventure and discovery.

189
00:24:47,840 --> 00:24:52,320
I hope your stay with us on this journey will be long and rewarding.

190
00:24:52,320 --> 00:25:04,240
And I hope you're still with us when we move into that promised day and age when we can look back and say that we were all pioneers in this headstrong field we've come to call holography.
